---
const axis='0 0 640 300';
---
<section class="ea-band" id="forecast-room"><h2>A decision before its outcome</h2><p class="ea-lead">Choose a reference procedure, record its forecast, then advance the information clock. The ledger retains the decision you actually made.</p>
<div class="ea-lab" data-ts-room>
<div class="ea-lab-heading"><div><h3>Texas residential electricity sales</h3><p>Fixed vintage · illustrative two-month release lag · million MWh</p></div><p class="ts-status" data-room-step>Loading fixed teaching data</p></div>
<figure class="ea-figure"><svg viewBox={axis} role="img" aria-label="Available sales history at the current forecast origin" data-room-chart></svg><figcaption data-room-clock></figcaption></figure>
<div class="ea-controls"><label>Procedure <select data-room-method><option value="naive">Seasonal naive</option><option value="ar">Expanding AR</option><option value="calendar">Calendar model, 60 months</option></select></label><p data-room-proposal></p><label>Why this choice? <input type="text" maxlength="400" data-room-reason placeholder="Name the assumption you are relying on" /></label><div class="ea-actions"><button class="ea-button" data-room-record>Record forecast</button><button class="ea-button secondary" data-room-reveal disabled>Reveal outcome</button><button class="ea-button secondary" data-room-next disabled>Next origin</button></div><p data-room-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></div>
<div class="ea-lab-foot"><p>Reference forecasts use admitted history. The 60-month calendar option is fixed throughout this room; the project separately selects its repair on a later boundary. This is ungraded exploration.</p><div class="ea-actions"><button class="ea-button secondary" data-room-export>Export decision ledger</button><button class="ea-button secondary" data-room-reset>Begin again</button></div><p class="ea-small">The ledger stays in this page's memory until you export it or leave. It is not uploaded.</p></div>
</div></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-experiment" id="persistence"><div><h2>Persistence and forecast risk</h2><p class="ea-lead">Change persistence and horizon. Compare a decaying shock with accumulated forecast uncertainty.</p><p><a href="/time-series/02-dynamics/">Derivation in Lecture 2 →</a></p></div><div class="ea-lab" data-ts-lab="ar"><figure class="ea-figure"><svg viewBox={axis} role="img" aria-label="AR shock response across horizons" data-ts-chart></svg><figcaption>Analytic AR(1) response to a unit shock. Innovation variance is one.</figcaption></figure><div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Persistence φ <output aria-label="Persistence φ" data-ts-phi-value></output><input type="range" min="-0.95" max="0.95" step="0.05" value="0.7" aria-label="Persistence φ" data-ts-phi /></label><label class="ea-control">Horizon <output aria-label="Horizon" data-ts-h-value></output><input type="range" min="1" max="24" step="1" value="4" aria-label="Horizon" data-ts-h /></label></div><div class="ea-lab-readouts"><div>Response at horizon h<strong data-ts-response></strong></div><div>Known-parameter error variance<strong data-ts-variance></strong></div></div><div class="ea-lab-foot"><p>Negative persistence alternates the response's sign. Variance remains nonnegative and uses squared response coefficients.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-experiment" id="future-filter"><div><h2>Can the future alter a past estimate?</h2><p class="ea-lead">Keep observations through date 20 fixed and change the next two records.</p><p><a href="/time-series/05-measurement/">Information audit in Lecture 5 →</a></p></div><div class="ea-lab" data-ts-lab="filter"><figure class="ea-figure"><svg viewBox={axis} role="img" aria-label="Illustrative series with fixed past and changing future records" data-ts-chart></svg><figcaption>A deterministic illustrative series. The dotted divider follows date 20.</figcaption></figure><div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Added to each of the next two observations <output aria-label="Change in future observations" data-ts-delta-value></output><input type="range" min="0" max="30" step="1" value="0" aria-label="Added to each of the next two observations" data-ts-delta /></label></div><div class="ea-lab-readouts"><div>Trailing estimate: change at date 20<strong data-ts-trailing></strong></div><div>Centered estimate: change at date 20<strong data-ts-centered></strong></div></div><div class="ea-lab-foot"><p>The centered five-period mean admits dates 21 and 22. The trailing three-period mean admits only dates 18–20.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-experiment" id="rotation"><div><h2>One covariance, several shock stories</h2><p class="ea-lead">Rotate a covariance factor while holding the observed covariance fixed.</p><p><a href="/time-series/10-identification/">Identification argument in Lecture 10 →</a></p></div><div class="ea-lab" data-ts-lab="rotation"><figure class="ea-figure"><svg viewBox={axis} role="img" aria-label="Impact vector of the first rotated structural shock" data-ts-chart></svg><figcaption>Impact of the first unit-variance shock. The covariance matrix has entries 4, 2, and 5.</figcaption></figure><div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Rotation angle, radians <output aria-label="Rotation angle in radians" data-ts-angle-value></output><input type="range" min="-3.14" max="3.14" step="0.02" value="0" aria-label="Rotation angle, radians" data-ts-angle /></label></div><div class="ea-lab-readouts"><div>Impact on the second variable<strong data-ts-impact></strong></div><div>Reconstructed cross-covariance<strong data-ts-covariance></strong></div></div><div class="ea-lab-foot"><p>Changing an impact pattern without changing covariance exposes an identification ambiguity. An economic shock needs additional assumptions.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="ea-band ea-experiment" id="variance"><div><h2>A surprise changes the next risk forecast</h2><p class="ea-lead">Hold the mean at zero and update a GARCH variance after a realized return surprise.</p><p><a href="/time-series/12-volatility/">Variance argument in Lecture 12 →</a></p></div><div class="ea-lab" data-ts-lab="garch"><figure class="ea-figure"><svg viewBox={axis} role="img" aria-label="Conditional variance forecasts converging toward the long-run variance" data-ts-chart></svg><figcaption>ω = 0.1, α = 0.1, β = 0.8; current variance = 1. Returns are in percentage points.</figcaption></figure><div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Latest return surprise <output aria-label="Latest return surprise" data-ts-shock-value></output><input type="range" min="-5" max="5" step="0.1" value="2" aria-label="Latest return surprise" data-ts-shock /></label></div><div class="ea-lab-readouts"><div>Next variance, percentage points squared<strong data-ts-next-variance></strong></div><div>Normal 5% return quantile, percentage points<strong data-ts-quantile></strong></div></div><div class="ea-lab-foot"><p>The normal quantile is conditional on a distributional assumption. The same variance with heavy-tailed shocks can imply a different risk threshold.</p></div></div></section>
<script>import {initializeTimeSeriesExperiments} from '../../scripts/time-series-experiments';initializeTimeSeriesExperiments();</script>
